":\"text\",\"q\":\"Death rates (overdose deaths per 100,000 population) were age-adjusted to the 2000 U.S. standard population\"}]},{\"v\":\"rate ratio\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"(2019 rate ratio = 1.06; 2020 rate ratio = 1.66)\"}]},{\"v\":\"absolute rate change\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"note\",\"q\":\"Absolute rate change is the difference between 2019 and 2020 rates.\"}]},{\"v\":\"relative rate change (%)\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"note\",\"q\":\"Relative rate change is the absolute rate change divided by the 2019 rate, multiplied by 100.\"}]},{\"v\":\"percentage\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"Evidence of naloxone administration was highest among AI/AN (21.5%) decedents\"}]}],\"regression_fields\":null,\"public_health_question\":{\"v\":\"How did drug overdose death rates change from 2019 to 2020 by race/ethnicity, sex and age, and how did they vary by county-level income inequality and treatment availability?\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"assesses differences in overdose death rates by county-level income inequality and availability of mental health treatment providers\"}]},\"conditions\":[{\"v\":\"drug overdose death\",\"role\":\"outcome\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"title\",\"q\":\"Drug Overdose Deaths, by Selected Sociodemographic and Social Determinants of Health Characteristics\"}]},{\"v\":\"opioid overdose death\",\"role\":\"outcome\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"In 2020, the rates of opioid-involved deaths among Black and AI/AN persons in counties with at least one opioid treatment program\"}]}],\"study_design\":[{\"v\":\"surveillance/descriptive\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"summary\",\"q\":\"Data from the State Unintentional Drug Overdose Reporting System (SUDORS) were used to analyze overdose death rates from 2019 to 2020\"}]},{\"v\":\"before/after or trend\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"This report describes changes in drug overdose death rates from 2019 to 2020\"}]},{\"v\":\"ecologic\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"was obtained from the 2021 County Health Rankings and analyzed by tertile.\"}]}],\"data_source_type\":[{\"v\":\"CDC surveillance system\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"summary\",\"q\":\"Data from the State Unintentional Drug Overdose Reporting System (SUDORS) were used to analyze overdose death rates from 2019 to 2020\"}]},{\"v\":\"other\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"The Drug Enforcement Administration’s controlled substance registration database was used to ascertain whether a county had at least one opioid treatment program\"}]}],\"data_source_named\":[{\"v\":\"State Unintentional Drug Overdose Reporting System (SUDORS)\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"were obtained from the State Unintentional Drug Overdose Reporting System (SUDORS).\"}]},{\"v\":\"2021 County Health Rankings\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"was obtained from the 2021 County Health Rankings and analyzed by tertile.\"}]},{\"v\":\"DEA controlled substance registration database\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"The Drug Enforcement Administration’s controlled substance registration database was used\"}]}],\"setting\":{\"v\":\"US subnational\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"title\",\"q\":\"25 States and the District of Columbia, 2019–2020\"}]},\"vaccine_outcome_type\":null,\"equity\":{\"level\":4,\"level_reason\":\"Racial/ethnic disparities in overdose rates are framed, quantified (rate ratios, 'nearly seven times', 'more than twice') and interpreted; income inequality and treatment access are analyzed as county-level determinants and stigma, criminalization and tribal clinic underfunding are named as causes.\",\"level_confidence\":\"high\",\"level_alternative\":{\"level\":3,\"reason\":\"Determinants are measured only as county-level tertiles shown side by side; one could read the structural explanations as cited background rather than analyzed.\"},\"dimensions\":[{\"v\":\"race/ethnicity\",\"role\":\"primary comparison\",\"basis\":\"assigned or observed\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"summary\",\"q\":\"Rates were examined by race and ethnicity and county-level social determinants of health (e.g., income inequality and treatment provider availability).\"}]},{\"v\":\"SES\",\"role\":\"stratifier\",\"ses_components\":[\"county-level income inequality ratio\"],\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"the overdose rate for counties with the highest income inequality (46.5) was more than twice that of counties with the lowest\"}]},{\"v\":\"age\",\"role\":\"stratifier\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"This report describes changes in drug overdose death rates from 2019 to 2020, stratified by sex, age group, and race and ethnicity.\"}]},{\"v\":\"sex\",\"role\":\"stratifier\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"When stratified by sex and age group, higher overdose death rates occurred among older Black males\"}]}],\"determinants_of_health\":{\"v\":\"income inequality; unequal access to substance use and mental health treatment; stigma; criminalization; mistrust in the health care system; underfunding of tribal clinics; transportation barriers\",\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"summary\",\"q\":\"social determinants of health, such as income inequality, exacerbate these inequities.\"},{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"More stigmatization, criminalization, and lack of access to evidence-based treatments among racial/ethnic minority groups with substance use disorders have been well-documented\"},{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"in AI/AN communities underfunding of tribal clinics has affected the availability of mental health treatment\"}]},\"equity_action\":{\"v\":false,\"ev\":[]},\"equity_measurement\":\"Rate ratios use White persons as referent. SES is an area measure (county income inequality ratio tertiles from County Health Rankings), not individual. Multiracial persons excluded; Hispanic not split by race; treatment availability measured at county level.\"},\"groupwise\":[{\"across\":[\"race/ethnicity\"],\"n_groups\":5,\"ordered\":false,\"assessment\":\"per-group vs referent only\",\"resolves_to_finer\":true,\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"table 1\",\"q\":\"Rate ratio is calculated by dividing the rate for persons of race/ethnicities other than White by the rate for White persons.\"},{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"Relative rate increases were highest among Black (44%) and AI/AN persons (39%)\"}]},{\"across\":[\"race/ethnicity\"],\"n_groups\":5,\"ordered\":false,\"assessment\":\"side by side\",\"resolves_to_finer\":false,\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"Evidence of injection drug use was most prevalent among White (28.0%) and AI/AN (22.9%) decedents.\"}]},{\"across\":[\"SES\"],\"n_groups\":3,\"ordered\":true,\"assessment\":\"side by side\",\"resolves_to_finer\":false,\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"In 2020, overdose death rates increased with increasing county-level income inequality ratios\"}]},{\"across\":[\"age\"],\"n_groups\":4,\"ordered\":true,\"assessment\":\"side by side\",\"resolves_to_finer\":false,\"ev\":[{\"loc\":\"text\",\"q\":\"Black persons aged 15–24 years experienced the largest relative rate increase from 2019 to 2020 (86%).\"}]}],\"uncertain_fields\":[\"study_design\",\"groupwise\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"notes\":\"Designs are not named; surveillance, trend and ecologic (county-tertile rates) are inferred. The CI-overlap test covers only 2019 vs 2020 within each group; race contrasts are rate ratios without inference. A residence check (>95% died in state of residence) is a data check; Supplementary tables and figures are not in the packet.\",\"view_issues\":\"The abstract claims 'significant disparities' across sex, age and race, but the only stated test is the 2019 vs 2020 CI overlap within groups. Table 1 labels marks 'p value <0.05' though no p-values are computed. The Discussion says AI/AN females were 'nearly twice' White females, but the stated 2020 rate ratio is 1.66.\"}", "type": "text"}]}